 {'title': 'Fixing an Out-of-Limits CG', 'img': 'PHAK_10-11_weight_shift_example', 'cite': 'PHAK 10-10, PHAK 10-11', 'code': 'AI.II.F.S2, AI.II.F.R6',
  'bullets': ['Weight moved / total weight', '= CG change / distance moved', 'PHAK: move 65 lb forward', 'Recheck after every change'],
  'notes': 'This proportion tells us how much weight to move. In the PHAK example, moving 65 pounds forward 120 inches brings the CG 1 inch forward to the aft limit.',
  'lp': {'teach': ['Weight shift formula: weight shifted divided by total weight equals change of CG divided by distance the weight is shifted (PHAK 10-11).',
     'PHAK example: total weight 7,800 lb, CG 81.5 inches, aft limit 80.5 inches. Cargo moves from station 150 to station 30, a 120 inch shift. Weight to be shifted / 7,800 = 1.0 / 120, so weight to be shifted = 65 lb (PHAK 10-11).',
     'Adding or removing weight uses: added weight / new total weight = change of CG / distance between the weight and the old CG. If the CG moves aft, add the change. If it moves forward, subtract it (PHAK 10-11).',
     'In the C172S, the practical fixes are moving bags forward, swapping heavier people to the front seats, or reducing weight (PHAK 10-3).'],
   'ask': ['The CG is too far aft. Name two ways to fix it in a C172S.']}},
